    "q-14337": {
      "dom_id": "step-q14337",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{4{d}^{2}-36}{2(d+3)}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The numerator is a difference of squares. It can be factored as\n\n$$\n4{d}^{2}-36=4({d}^{2}-9)=4(d+3)(d-3).\n$$\n\nCanceling out any common factors that appear in the numerator and the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{d}^{2}-36}{2(d+3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(d+3)(d-3)}{2(d+3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{2(d+3)}(d-3)}{\\cancel{2(d+3)}} & = \\\\ 2(d-3).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
